  <h2 id="meaning">What does cum laude mean?</h2>
  <p>Cum laude is Latin for “with praise”, and it’s often translated as “with honor”. The other two honors add a word that raises the praise:</p>
  <table class="guide-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Honor</th><th scope="col">Meaning</th><th scope="col">Rank</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td>Summa cum laude</td><td>With highest praise, or with highest honor</td><td>Highest</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Magna cum laude</td><td>With great praise, or with great honor</td><td>Second</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Cum laude</td><td>With praise, or with honor</td><td>Third</td></tr>
  </tbody></table>
  <p>The honor is usually noted on the diploma and the transcript, and graduates are often recognized at commencement, for example with an honor cord. Latin honors are separate from departmental honors and honors programs, which have their own requirements.</p>

  <h3 id="magna">What is magna cum laude?</h3>
  <p>Magna cum laude means “with great praise”. It’s the middle of the three Latin honors, above cum laude and below summa cum laude. At the colleges compared below it takes a GPA of 3.5 to 3.75.</p>
  <h3 id="summa">What is summa cum laude?</h3>
  <p>Summa cum laude means “with highest praise”. It’s the highest Latin honor, and at the colleges compared below it takes a GPA of 3.8 to 3.95. Colleges that rank by class honor only the top few percent this way.</p>

  <h2 id="highest">Is magna cum laude the highest honor?</h2>
  <p>No. Summa cum laude is the highest Latin honor, magna cum laude is second, and cum laude is third. Magna cum laude is still a strong result, well above a B+ average.</p>

  <h2 id="gpa">What GPA is cum laude, magna cum laude and summa cum laude?</h2>
  <p>There’s no national rule. Each college sets its own cutoffs on the 4.0 scale, and they differ by a few tenths. These are the cutoffs five colleges’ registrars publish:</p>
  <table class="guide-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">College</th><th scope="col">Cum laude</th><th scope="col">Magna cum laude</th><th scope="col">Summa cum laude</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td><a href={sources.utah}>University of Utah</a></td><td>3.5</td><td>3.7</td><td>3.9</td></tr>
    <tr><td><a href={sources.usc}>University of South Carolina</a></td><td>3.5</td><td>3.75</td><td>3.95</td></tr>
    <tr><td><a href={sources.uic}>University of Illinois Chicago</a></td><td>3.5</td><td>3.75</td><td>3.9</td></tr>
    <tr><td><a href={sources.usm}>University of Southern Mississippi</a></td><td>3.5</td><td>3.7</td><td>3.85</td></tr>
    <tr><td><a href={sources.ccny}>City College of New York</a></td><td>3.2</td><td>3.5</td><td>3.8</td></tr>
  </tbody></table>
  <p>Among these five, cum laude takes a 3.5 at four and a 3.2 at City College of New York, and summa cum laude takes 3.8 to 3.95. Your college’s catalog or registrar’s website has its exact numbers.</p>
