  <h2 id="percentile">Colleges that award honors by class rank</h2>
  <p>Some colleges don’t publish a fixed GPA. They honor a share of each graduating class instead, so the GPA needed changes from year to year and between schools within the college.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><a href={sources.utd}>UT Dallas</a> gives summa cum laude to the top 5% of graduates, magna cum laude to the next 10% and cum laude to the next 15%, with a 3.40 GPA minimum. For Fall 2025 to Summer 2026, summa cum laude took a 3.991 in Natural Sciences and Mathematics and a 3.935 in the Jindal School of Management.</li>
    <li><a href={sources.uci}>UC Irvine</a> honors no more than 16% of graduates: about 2% summa cum laude, 4% magna cum laude and 10% cum laude. For 2025–26, cum laude took between 3.742 and 3.929, depending on the school.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>At colleges like these, a 3.7 may not be enough for any Latin honor, so check the latest published cutoffs rather than a general rule.</p>

  <h2 id="requirements">What else do you need besides the GPA?</h2>
  <p>A high GPA isn’t always enough. Colleges can add conditions like these:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Credits at the college.</strong> The University of Utah requires at least 60 semester hours there, 50 of them letter-graded. The University of South Carolina requires 60 hours in residence, and the University of Illinois Chicago awards honors on the GPA earned at UIC alone, so transfer grades don’t count there.</li>
    <li><strong>Letter grades.</strong> Pass/fail courses aren’t part of the GPA, and some colleges set a minimum number of graded hours. UT Dallas asks for at least 45.</li>
    <li><strong>Rounding.</strong> A 3.495 isn’t always a 3.5. Colleges differ on whether they round a GPA before comparing it with a cutoff, so check your registrar’s rule if you’re close.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="resume">How do you write cum laude on a résumé?</h2>
  <p>Put the honor after your degree, in lowercase: “Bachelor of Science in Biology, magna cum laude”. Many people italicize it, as with other Latin phrases. List it only once it has been awarded, and use the exact honor on your transcript.</p>

  <h2 id="other-countries">Latin honors outside the US</h2>
  <p>The same Latin terms are used in other countries, often differently. German doctorates, for instance, are graded summa cum laude, magna cum laude, cum laude or rite (passed), so there cum laude is the third of four grades rather than an extra honor.</p>

